// GENERATED from pl-headless lib/study-concepts.ts: the Psyched Study's sixteen concepts and the three continuations, word for word.
struct StudyConcept: Identifiable, Hashable { let roman: String; let title: String; let body: String; let law: String; let prompt: String; let acknowledgment: String; var id: String { roman } }

enum StudyCurriculum {
    static let concepts: [StudyConcept] = [
        StudyConcept(roman: "I", title: "Attention Is Construction, Not Observation.", body: "Your brain builds predictive models of reality. Consistent attention is the architectural input to those models. You are not a witness to your life — you are its engineer. The Reticular Activating System filters reality based on what you have told it matters through sustained, repeated attention. You are not attracting. You are training perception.", law: "Original Territory", prompt: "This past week — not what you intended to think about, not what you wanted to give your attention to — what actually kept returning? What thoughts showed up without being invited, over and over? Name them specifically.", acknowledgment: "What returns without invitation is already building. That is the first concept running in your life before you named it."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "II", title: "The Identity Precedes the Action.", body: "\"Prepared for luxury\" is not a wish. It is a configuration statement about who you already are in relation to what is arriving. You cannot behave your way into becoming someone different. You decide who you are first — fully, without condition — and behavior reorganizes around that declaration as the only logical outcome.", law: "Truth I", prompt: "There is something you keep trying to do but have not done consistently. Before you name the obstacles, answer honestly: in the part of your mind that operates below intention, do you actually believe you are the kind of person who does that thing? Write what comes first, before you edit it.", acknowledgment: "The belief beneath the behavior is the real architecture. Naming it is the first act of changing it."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "III", title: "Resistance Is a Blueprint, Not a Verdict.", body: "The places where you feel most blocked are not evidence of failure. They are structural data about the shape of what you are building. Friction is the outline of the form. Every creative person who has worked on anything real already knows this intuitively. Most people have not been told they are allowed to apply it to their entire life.", law: "Original Territory", prompt: "Where are you feeling the most friction right now — in work, in creative practice, in a relationship, in a decision you keep avoiding? Do not describe how it makes you feel. Describe what it actually looks like, in specific, concrete terms.", acknowledgment: "What you just named is not an obstacle. It is a blueprint. The friction has a shape."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "IV", title: "Wishing Is Episodic. Persisting Is Structural.", body: "The brain builds new defaults through repetition across time, not conviction in a moment. Duration of presence matters categorically more than intensity of belief. You can want something desperately for one hour and nothing permanent happens. You can want something moderately for seven years and it becomes the architecture of your life.", law: "Truth III", prompt: "Name one thing you have wanted for a long time. Then count honestly — not estimate — how many days in the last thirty did you do something, anything, even small, in the direction of building toward it. What did those days look like? What did the other days look like?", acknowledgment: "The count is the data. What the days looked like is the practice."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "V", title: "The Observer Rewrites the Observed.", body: "Your internal narrative about yourself functions as a perceptual filter on every experience that follows. Changing the story is not performance — it is recalibrating the instrument you use to interpret everything. The new story does not erase old evidence. It changes what new evidence you are capable of seeing.", law: "Truth II", prompt: "There is something you have decided is true about yourself — a conclusion you reached at some point that you now carry as a fact. Write it plainly, the way you would say it to yourself when no one else is listening.", acknowledgment: "The fact that you examined it means it is no longer fully operating in the dark. That is the beginning of this concept working."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "VI", title: "Control Is Input Management, Not Outcome Forcing.", body: "Truth II is not about dictating the form of what arrives. It is about maintaining dominion over your inputs: what you attend to, what you repeat, what you call yourself, what you feed. The output organizes around inputs. The inputs are entirely yours. The exact form of the output is not.", law: "Truth II", prompt: "Think of something you are trying to make happen right now. Write two lists without organizing first: what in this situation do you actually have control over, and what are you spending energy on that you do not control. See what ratio you end up with.", acknowledgment: "The ratio you found is honest information. Most people never make that list."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "VII", title: "Creation as Proof, Not Performance.", body: "Every piece of work you make is empirical evidence that the philosophy is operational. The archive is not a portfolio assembled for an audience. It is documentation that someone converted internal state into external reality, repeatedly, over years, regardless of who was watching. Seven years of physical output is not a spiritual claim. It is a case study.", law: "Original Territory", prompt: "What have you made, built, written, produced, or created — at any scale, for any audience including an audience of one — that still exists in the world right now? It does not need to be impressive or finished. What is it, and roughly when did you make it?", acknowledgment: "What you named exists. That is not a small thing. The archive starts with one made thing."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "VIII", title: "The Compound Effect of Invisibility.", body: "The season where you are building and nothing is visible yet is not failure. It is the structural phase — the most important one. Buildings do not begin at the ground floor. The people who leave during invisibility will never know what they were building. The people who stay carry the evidence of everything.", law: "Original Territory", prompt: "Is there something you stopped working on before anyone could see it? Or something you are in the middle of right now that nobody around you knows about or understands yet? Write what it is. If you stopped — write what that moment actually felt like.", acknowledgment: "The invisible phase has a name now. Naming it changes what it is."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "IX", title: "Beyond Neville Goddard: The Assumption Must Be Made Physical.", body: "Neville was right that living from the end is a genuine cognitive reframe. His gap was passivity. A false self-concept can be maintained in meditation indefinitely. It cannot be maintained while surrounded by seven years of your own physical output. The archive is the wish fulfilled made into a record that argues for itself.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "There is something you believe about your future that exists entirely inside your mind right now — no physical evidence of it exists yet. Write what that thing is. Then write one thing small enough to do this week that would be the first piece of physical evidence it is real. Not a plan. A made thing.", acknowledgment: "The first physical evidence is the hardest to create. After that, the argument has already begun."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "X", title: "Beyond Napoleon Hill: Durability, Not Burning.", body: "Hill identified persistence and burning desire as operative forces. The error: burning is episodic — it exhausts itself. Replace burning desire with persisting attention. The archive exists not because someone burned. It exists because someone kept going when the fire was low.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "Think of the last time your motivation for something that genuinely mattered dropped significantly — not permanent quitting, just the fire going low. What did you do during that period? Did you keep going at a reduced level, or stop? If you kept going, what did that look like when there was no energy behind it?", acknowledgment: "What you did when the fire was low is the most honest answer you have given. That is where the practice actually lives."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XI", title: "Beyond James Allen: The Persistent Thought, Not the Virtuous One.", body: "Allen taught that outer circumstances reflect inner thinking. Correct structurally. His limitation was moral framing. The thoughts that shape your reality are not the noble ones. They are the persistent ones. You are not what you think in your best moments. You are what you think when nothing is working and no one is watching.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "What runs in your mind when you are not directing it — in the space before sleep, in idle moments, when you are waiting without a screen? Not what you wish ran. What actually occupies the automatic channel? Write it honestly. No one else is here.", acknowledgment: "The automatic channel is the real curriculum you have been running. Now you know what you are working with."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XII", title: "Beyond Bob Proctor: Evidence Shifts Paradigms Faster Than Repetition.", body: "Proctor's insight: subconscious paradigm governs behavior, and behavior change without paradigm change is temporary. A new affirmation can be argued with internally at 3am. A seven-year archive cannot. Creation produces proof, not assertion. Proof is the more powerful paradigm-shifter.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "What is one belief about yourself that you have been trying to think your way out of? Now look at your actual life for evidence that already contradicts it. Not hope for future evidence. Evidence that already exists, that you may not have been looking at directly. Write what you find.", acknowledgment: "Evidence that already exists and has not been looked at directly is the most available resource you have."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XIII", title: "Beyond Abraham Hicks: The Vortex Is a Practice, Not a Destination.", body: "Aligned emotional state amplifies your attention economy — that is real. What the vortex framing got wrong: it implies a state you achieve and then receive from. There is no arrival. There is only the maintenance of the practice. You never reach alignment once. You maintain it daily.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "What does alignment actually feel like in a normal day for you — not a peak day, a Wednesday? What are the specific conditions, activities, or states where you feel most like yourself? Honestly: how many of the last seven days included those conditions?", acknowledgment: "The specific conditions are the practice. The gap between knowing them and creating them daily is where this concept lives."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XIV", title: "Beyond Earl Nightingale: Creation Is a More Complete Form of Thinking.", body: "\"We become what we think about\" is the third truth in five words. The extension: creation is thinking with your body, your time, your hands, and your decisions simultaneously. The Codex is not a record of thought. It is thought converted into physical form, repeatedly, across years.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "Think of the last time you made something — wrote, drew, built, cooked, arranged, recorded, anything. What were you thinking about while your hands were working? What did the act of making clarify that just thinking about the subject had not?", acknowledgment: "What the hands clarified that the mind had not — that is this concept operating in your own experience."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XV", title: "Beyond Florence Scovel Shinn: Language Encodes the Filter.", body: "Language is generative because it encodes the narrative frame through which you process every subsequent experience. \"Prepared for luxury\" is not a prayer. It configures the perceptual system before the thing arrives. The most powerful language is what you use to name what you are building. The most powerful naming is making it physical.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "How do you describe yourself when someone asks what you do, or what you are working on? Write the exact words you actually use — not the impressive version, not the humble version, the automatic one. Then write what would be true to say — the version that names what you are actually building without performing it.", acknowledgment: "The automatic version and the true version are two different instruments. You now know the difference between them."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XVI", title: "Beyond Wallace Wattles: The Substance Is Not Formless, It Is Material.", body: "Wattles reached for formless thinking substance to explain why consistent thought produces outcomes. Neuroscience now confirms: the brain's predictive modeling system builds toward what it consistently imagines. You impress upon material reality directly through consistent creative output. The substance is watercolor paper, cotton garments, composition books, and a seven-year archive. Not formless. Undeniable.", law: "The Evolution", prompt: "You have moved through all sixteen. One year from today, what will exist in the world — physically, materially, in some form that can be held or heard or seen or read — that does not exist right now? Write it as a statement of what will be, not what you hope might be.", acknowledgment: "One year from today. You wrote it as what will be. That is the practice. That is all sixteen operating simultaneously."),
    ]
    static let continuations: [StudyConcept] = [
        StudyConcept(roman: "XVII", title: "The Principles Were Always Structural, Not Spiritual.", body: "Reality operates by pattern, and pattern repeats at every scale. What the Hermetic tradition called cosmic law, neuroscience now calls predictive modeling. What it called correspondence, systems theory calls fractal self-similarity. The principles were not handed down — they were observed, recorded, and confirmed across every domain of human knowledge. The mystical language around it was the era, not the insight.", law: "The Foundation", prompt: "Correspondence says what exists at one scale exists at all scales. Look at one pattern that repeats in your life across different contexts — the same dynamic in your creative work and your relationships, the same resistance in your finances and your physical practice. Name the pattern and where it shows up in more than one place.", acknowledgment: "The pattern you named is not coincidence. It is correspondence — the same architecture running at multiple scales simultaneously."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XVIII", title: "Love of Self Is Not Maintenance. It Is the Prerequisite.", body: "You cannot build toward a life that is genuinely yours without an honest relationship with yourself. Self-love here is not bubble baths and affirmations. It is the discipline of honest self-observation without judgment, treating your own needs as data rather than inconvenience, and the willingness to want what you actually want. The identity that precedes the action has to be one you actually inhabit. Inhabitation requires love. The real thing.", law: "The Inner Architecture", prompt: "What do you consistently deprioritize about your own needs in the name of building, working, or producing? And honestly: what would change about what you are building if you treated that deprioritized need as load-bearing rather than optional?", acknowledgment: "What you named as last is telling you where the foundation is thin. The build cannot stand permanently on ground you are unwilling to tend."),
        StudyConcept(roman: "XIX", title: "The Thought Does Not Attract. It Configures.", body: "A thought held consistently becomes an expectation. An expectation configures your perceptual system — what you notice, what you filter out. Your configured perception shapes your behavior. Your consistent behavior produces outcomes. The thought did not broadcast a signal and receive a delivery. It trained the instrument you use to move through the world. The magnetism is mechanical, not mystical — which means it is more reliable, not less.", law: "The Mechanism", prompt: "What are you currently expecting — not hoping, not wanting, expecting as the default assumption — about how something important in your life will unfold? Expectation is quieter than desire. Write what you find when you look for the expectation rather than the wish.", acknowledgment: "The expectation you named is the actual magnetic field. What it is pointed toward is what the system is currently configured to build."),
    ]
}
